{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-71100",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{(\\frac{6}{7})}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
